.\" The .TH line is a placeholder: packaging/stamp.sh rewrites its date and
.\" version at build time, so neither is kept current here by hand.
.TH HI 1 "2026-09-07" "say-hi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hi \- your shell config, on every host you say hi to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hi
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RB [ \-\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-mux | \-\-no\-mux ]
.I target
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B hi
.br
.B hi \-\-doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B hi \-\-install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-link " " {none|user|system}]
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-configure
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-uninstall
.RB [ \-\-purge ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-update
.RI [ tag ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-preview
.RB { colors | packages | header | targets }
.br
.BR "hi \-h" " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hi
copies your
.B say-hi
configuration (prompt, colors, aliases, editor configs, connect header) to a
target, starts a session there, and removes every trace when the session ends,
abrupt disconnect included. A say-hi installed on the target is neither read
nor touched.
.PP
With a trailing
.IR command ,
hi runs it inside its session and exits: the command gets hi's aliases, PATH,
and environment, and a pty when your stdin is one. Only the command's output
goes to stdout; hi's progress and errors go to stderr. For a plain, pty\-free
remote command (a tarball piped into a file, say), use
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
An ssh target with neither
.BR base64 (1)
nor
.BR openssl (1),
or nowhere to make a
.BR mktemp (1)
directory, gets its own login session instead, with a notice saying why.
.SH SESSION SHELL
Your login shell when hi styles it (bash, zsh, or fish), else the first of
fish > zsh > bash on the target. A target with no bash gets the first of
fish > zsh > dash > ash > sh, with hi's aliases (and in dash, ash, or sh a
colored prompt) but no header.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
.I target
is tried against each backend in order; the first match wins:
.TP
.B 1. ssh host
a literal
.B Host
entry in
.B ~/.ssh/config
(wildcards do not count).
.TP
.B 2. container
a running container, by name or ID, on the first of docker, podman, nerdctl,
and finch that has it; docker and podman also take a compose service name
with one running container.
.TP
.B 3. nomad allocation
a running allocation, by ID or ID prefix.
.TP
.B 4. kubernetes pod
a running pod in the current
.BR kubectl (1)
context and namespace, or
.I namespace:pod
or
.I context:namespace:pod
for one elsewhere.
.PP
.I alloc/task
and
.I pod/container
pick one task or container
.RB ( "nomad alloc exec \-task" ,
.BR "kubectl exec \-c" ).
Without it
.BR nomad (1)
refuses a multi\-task allocation and
.BR kubectl (1)
takes the pod's first container with a warning. Completion offers the pairs
and the prefixed pod names.
.PP
A name matching none of these still goes to
.BR ssh (1),
so unlisted hosts work.
.BR \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR
skips every probe: the fix for a container that shadows an ssh host of the
same name, or the other way around.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
print the usage summary (synopsis, target resolution, hi's options) and exit.
Answered anywhere ahead of the target, never passed to
.BR ssh (1);
a word after it is refused.
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
print the version (the packaging stamp, or
.B git describe
in a checkout), then the kind of tree (checkout, package, or tree) and its
path, and exit. The one ssh short option hi claims, since a bug report asks
for it first; a word after it is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-use \fIbackend\fR
force that arm and skip every probe:
.B ssh
(ssh's own error speaks if the host is not there), or one of
.BR docker ,
.BR podman ,
.BR nerdctl ,
.BR finch ,
.BR nomad ,
and
.BR kube .
Any other name, or two different ones, is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
a bare shell on whatever
.I target
resolves to: no copy, no aliases, no header. The target needs only a shell
(and
.BR sshd (8)
for ssh) \- no
.BR tar (1),
no
.BR base64 (1),
no writable
.B /tmp
or
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B \-\-mux
run the session in a local
.BR tmux (1),
.BR zellij (1),
or
.BR screen (1)
session, the first on your PATH, named
.BI hi\- target
(characters tmux rejects become dashes), so a dropped link leaves it running
and the next
.B hi \-\-mux
to that target reattaches. Client\-side only. Inside tmux hi switches to the
session; inside screen or zellij it opens a window or tab. With no terminal,
or no multiplexer, hi connects as usual.
.B _HI_MUX=1
makes this the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
connect without the multiplexer, past
.B _HI_MUX=1
or an earlier
.BR \-\-mux ;
the last of the two flags wins.
.PP
The local commands act on this machine instead of connecting. Each needs
.BR scripts/ ,
which a package ships and a session does not, so inside a session each says
so and stops.
.B \-\-update
also needs
.BR .git ,
which a package has not.
.BR \-n " is " \-\-dry\-run 's
short form wherever it appears.
.TP
.B \-\-preview \fR{\fBcolors\fR|\fBpackages\fR|\fBheader\fR|\fBtargets\fR}
print one of these and exit:
.B colors
\- each user and ssh host a pin, tag, or pattern colors, in that color, with
the rule it matched;
.B packages
\- the package\-priority legend, marking the ranks the floor hides;
.B header
\- the connect header at your settings;
.B targets
\- every target
.B hi <TAB>
offers and its backend, probed past the completion cache.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-use\fR \fIbackend\fR] [\fIssh\-options\fR] [\fItarget\fR]
print a read\-only pre\-flight report instead of connecting: the local tree;
the config overlay; every rc and overlay shell file, parsed by the shell that
reads it; the install (each shell's rc lines and whether they name this tree,
the
.B hi
link, what
.B hi
on PATH runs, the file a login bash or a ZDOTDIR zsh really reads); every
backend, probed and timed; and, with a
.IR target ,
the backend it resolves to plus an ssh reachability check. ssh options reach
that check's BatchMode probe as they would a connect, and are reported as
ignored for a non\-ssh target.
.B \-\-use
forces the backend as a connect would.
.BR \-\-plain ,
.BR \-\-mux ,
and
.B \-\-no\-mux
are ignored, so a connect line pastes behind
.BR \-\-doctor .
.B \-\-json
prints the same rows as one JSON document on stdout, with the finding count
as
.BR findings .
.TP
.B \-\-install \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-link\fR {\fBnone\fR|\fBuser\fR|\fBsystem\fR}] [\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
add or repair say-hi's lines in the bash, zsh, and fish rc files (each shell
only if installed; on macOS also a
.B ~/.bash_profile
line so a login bash reads
.BR ~/.bashrc ),
link
.BR hi ,
and open the
.B \-\-configure
menu at a terminal. An rc file that fails its syntax check stops the install:
with a question at a terminal, outright without one;
.BR \-y / \-\-yes
installs anyway.
.B \-\-link
places the link:
.B user
.RB ( ~/.local/bin/hi ,
the default),
.B system
.RB ( /usr/bin/hi ,
through sudo), or
.B none
(the wired shells alias
.B hi
regardless; the link is for scripts). No link is made when a
.B hi
on PATH already runs this tree, and a link owned by a package or anyone else
is left alone.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
answers the menu as under
.BR \-\-configure .
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints every write (rc lines, settings.sh, the link) and makes none.
.B scripts/install.sh \-\-prefix
.I dir
is packaging mode: stage the tree under
.I dir
with
.B /usr/bin/hi
and
.BR /etc/profile.d/say\-hi.sh ,
touching no home directory.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-purge\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
remove those rc lines (and a macOS
.B ~/.bash_profile
the install created, if nothing else was written to it), settings.sh, and a
.B ~/.local/bin/hi
or
.B /usr/bin/hi
link that points at this tree.
.B \-\-purge
removes the whole overlay
.RB ( ~/.config/say-hi )
too. The tree and the one\-time
.B .hi\-orig
rc backups stay.
.TP
.B \-\-configure \fR[\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
edit the settings alone, leaving the rc wiring and the link be: a numbered
menu under a live preview of the header and prompt, grouped as Features,
Header (its items to toggle and reorder, header presets, width, package check
depth, hidden addresses), Prompt, and Advanced.
.B p
picks a preset
.RB ( everything ,
.BR balanced ,
.BR minimal ),
.B s
saves, and
.B q
quits without writing; nothing is written before either.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
applies one without the menu;
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the settings.sh it would write and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-update \fR[\fItag\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
fetch the tags and check out the newest release, detached, or
.I tag
to move forward or back. A tree with uncommitted changes is refused. Releases
are tags only; to follow a branch, run
.B git pull
in the checkout.
.B \-\-dry\-run
fetches and names the tag without moving. Needs a checkout: a package has no
.BR .git .
.PP
Everything else is passed through to
.BR ssh (1)
unchanged
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-o ,
and the rest). ssh has no option starting with two dashes, so an unknown one
is hi's error. An option that opens on a word takes it joined too
.RB ( \-\-use=docker ,
.BR \-\-preview=colors ,
.BR \-\-update=v1.0.0 );
one that opens on a switch or takes nothing refuses it
.RB ( \-\-doctor=json ,
.BR \-\-plain=1 ).
The first non\-option word is the target and everything after it is the
remote command, so
.B hi host ls \-la
reads as it does for ssh; one of hi's own options there is refused by name.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B a connect
the session's or remote command's own status, as
.BR ssh (1)
would return it. 1 when hi refuses before connecting: an unknown or misplaced
option, a bad or conflicting
.BR \-\-use ,
hi's own options with no target, or a local command inside a session.
ssh options with no target return what
.BR ssh (1)
does.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
0 with nothing to report, 1 on any finding; the count is in the report (and
.BR \-\-json 's
.BR findings ),
never in the status.
.TP
.B the other local commands
0 on success, 1 otherwise; an rc file failing its syntax check stops
.B \-\-install
at 1 before it writes anything.
.SH SESSION COMMANDS
.TP
.B hi_info
an alias, in a session and in the wired local shells, printing where the
running say\-hi lives: its home, root, and launcher. A diagnostic, not part
of the 1.x contract.
.SH COMPLETION
bash, zsh, and fish all complete from one lister,
.BR common/targets.sh .
.TP
.B hi <TAB>
every target: the
.B Host
entries in
.B ~/.ssh/config
plus every running container, allocation, and pod, each with its backend's
symbol (the
.B _HI_SYMBOL_*
settings; bash shows it only when bash 4 or later lists the matches). The
list is reused for five seconds, and each backend gets two seconds to answer.
zsh colors it by backend (ssh hosts yellow, containers blue, nomad green,
kubernetes light purple) through the
.B hi\-targets
tag's
.B list\-colors
style, which one of your own replaces.
.TP
.B hi \-\-<TAB>
hi's options, with their one\-line help where the shell has a column for it
(fish and zsh), probing no backend. A session is offered none of the local
commands, and a package install no
.BR \-\-update .
.TP
.B hi \-\-install \-\-<TAB>
behind a local command, that command's own switches;
.B \-\-use
completes the backends (on a connect too),
.B \-\-link
and
.B \-\-preset
their values, and
.B hi \-\-update <TAB>
the release tags a checkout knows of.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives outside the tree, in
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
(under
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
when set), and is carried to every target; the files are listed under
.BR FILES .
.B hi \-\-configure
writes
.BR settings.sh ;
you create the rest, the tree's
.B settings/
copy of a file being the default until you copy it here. Every setting is an
environment variable, tabulated with its default in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md .
The tree itself can be root\-owned (a package install): hi never writes into
it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/say-hi
the tree, in a package install; a git checkout (in a directory named
.BR say-hi )
works the same.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh
settings, written by
.BR "hi \-\-configure" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/colors, ~/.config/say-hi/packages
your copies of
.B settings/colors
and
.BR settings/packages ,
used in place of the tree's.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/packages.d/
more packages files, each a group the check paints after
.B packages
in name order, in the color its
.B color=
line names.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/vim.rc, init.lua, nano.rc, emacs.el
replacements for vim's, neovim's, nano's, and emacs's configs, used whole.
Without one, hi carries the config each editor already reads here
.RB ( ~/.vimrc ,
.BR ~/.config/nvim/init.lua ,
.BR ~/.nanorc ,
.BR ~/.emacs ,
and their alternates), else the tree's. A line that reads a file no target
has is dropped and named by
.BR \-\-doctor ;
.B _HI_INCLUDES=keep
sends it anyway, as does a
.B # hi-allow
line above it. tmux, micro, and the shell and prompt files below get the same
pass.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/tmux.conf, micro/
the same for tmux (its alias becomes
.BR "tmux \-f" )
and for micro's
.BR settings.json ,
.BR bindings.json ,
and
.B init.lua
(read through
.BR \-config\-dir ).
Without them hi carries
.B ~/.tmux.conf
and micro's own config directory.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/aliases.sh
your own aliases, sourced after hi's, so one of the same name replaces hi's.
.BR _HI_*_OPTS ,
.BR _HI_*_BIN ,
and
.B _HI_DISABLE_*
values set here arrive too late: they belong in
.BR settings.sh .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/plugins.d/
plugins in the aliases.sh subset, sourced in name order after the aliases and
before the prompt; one a shell cannot parse is skipped with a warning. A
plugin's
.B _HI_SEGMENT
command is drawn in the prompt.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/bash.sh, zsh.zsh, config.fish
your per\-shell preferences, sourced after hi's own so yours win.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/oh-my-posh.json, oh-my-posh.yaml, oh-my-posh.toml
oh\-my\-posh's config, carried to every target as its
.B POSH_CONFIG
when oh\-my\-posh draws the prompt there. Without one, the file
.B POSH_CONFIG
or an rc's
.B oh\-my\-posh init \-\-config
names is carried instead.
.TP
.B ~/.config/starship.toml, ~/.config/bat/config, ~/.config/eza/theme.yml
the starship, bat, and eza configs in force here (or wherever
.BR STARSHIP_CONFIG ,
.BR BAT_CONFIG_PATH ,
or
.B EZA_CONFIG_DIR
points), carried to every target \- starship's only when starship is a
prompt program a target is handed. The aliases then leave out bat's theme
flag. A
.BR starship.toml ,
.BR bat.conf ,
or
.B theme.yml
in
.B ~/.config/say-hi
is carried instead.
.TP
.B ~/.p10k.zsh, ~/.zshrc, ~/.bashrc, ~/.config/fish/fish_variables
read for the prompt programs a target is handed: powerlevel10k's config, the
oh\-my\-zsh and oh\-my\-bash theme files
.B ZSH_THEME
and
.B OSH_THEME
name, and tide's
.B tide_*
universal variables, carried as
.BR p10k.zsh ,
.BR omz-theme.zsh ,
.BR omb-theme.sh ,
and
.BR tide.vars ,
each replaced by a copy of that name in
.BR ~/.config/say-hi .
Unset,
.B _HI_PROMPT_TOOL
hands the prompt to every such program installed here;
.B _HI_PROMPT_TOOL=hi
keeps hi's own.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every setting is an environment variable (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
A few that matter here:
.TP
.B _HI_HOME
the directory holding the
.B say-hi
tree: derived from hi's own path when unset, and set by
.B /etc/profile.d/say-hi.sh
in a package install.
.TP
.B _HI_TRUECOLOR
.B 1
or
.BR 0 :
whether your terminal renders 24\-bit color. Unset, it is read off
.B COLORTERM
and shipped to the session, since ssh never forwards that.
.TP
.B _HI_MUX
.B 1
makes every connect a
.B \-\-mux
one;
.B \-\-no\-mux
overrides it once. Client\-side only.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B hi prod-db
a session on the ssh host
.BR prod\-db ,
with your prompt, colors, and aliases.
.TP
.B hi -J bastion internal 'df -h'
run a command on
.B internal
through a jump host.
.TP
.B hi 8f41c2
a session in the running container or allocation with that ID.
.TP
.B hi \-\-use docker web
the container
.BR web ,
even with an ssh host of the same name; no probe runs.
.TP
.B hi \-\-plain rescue
a bare shell on
.BR rescue ,
nothing copied: for a box with no writable
.B /tmp
or no
.BR tar (1).
.TP
.B hi \-\-doctor \-\-json prod-db > report.json
the pre\-flight report as a document for a bug report; the exit status says
whether anything was found.
.TP
.B hi
no target: print the usage summary and exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Every setting is in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ;
the README and the rest of the docs are at
.IR https://github.com/ivylikethevine/say-hi .
